WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.401
-We're here with
Anderson Cooper.

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:03.970
So, this book is about
your mom's side of the family.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.705
-You know, I was told that
you guys didn't have a copy

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.374
of the book so I actually
stopped at Barnes &amp; Noble

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:09.309
down the block
as I was coming here,

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:12.245
ran in, bought a copy,
and then came here.

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.347
-That's so embarrassing.
You bought your own book?

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.115
-Yes, I did. Yes.

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:17.850
I got a text on the way like,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.285
"Um, do you have a copy
of the book with you?

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:20.319
Because they don't have one."

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:22.054
I'm like, "What,
like I walk around

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:23.556
with my book like a schmuck?"

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.191
-Did you find it right away
or did you have --

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:26.760
-I just happen to walk around
the city like,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.295
"Hey, Anderson Cooper's
got a new book."

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:33.199
-The worst would be if
you walked around like this.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.034
Just, like, to make sure
people saw it was you.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.770
This is your mom's side
of the family.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:37.771
-Yes.
-The Vanderbilts.

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:38.838
-Yes.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.340
-For those who don't know
the history...

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.477
-Yeah, they -- This guy,
Commodore Cornelius Vanderbilt,

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.747
made a ton of money
in the late 1800s.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.448
He started with nothing

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:54.320
and built a steamship empire and
then a railroad empire.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.789
You can see a statue of him
outside Grand Central Station,

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:58.023
which, by the way,
he built for himself.

00:00:58.023 --> 00:00:59.459
-Oh, that's very nice.

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:02.561
When you got a little extra
scratch lying around, why not?

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:04.830
-It wasn't like a grateful city
built a statue for him.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.499
He was like, "You know what?
I'll do it myself.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:07.967
Don't worry about it. I got it.
I got this."

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.535
-Much like you, he actually went

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:12.171
to the statue store
and bought it.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:14.940
He was like,
"Do you have a Cornelius?

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.142
Carried it out, walking around.

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.611
-And, so, the book is
really just about this --

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.780
I mean, when he died, he was
the richest man in the world.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.817
He had 1 out of every 20 dollars
that was in circulation was his.

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:27.186
And, yeah, now I'm wondering
where it went.

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:30.089
But that's what the book
is really about.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:31.624
It's about -- I never wanted
anything to do with

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.758
that side of the family.

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:35.127
I felt like
no good could come with it.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:38.064
And I was really interested in
sort of how the pathology,

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.433
his money obsession,
how it infected

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.368
subsequent generations
in the family.

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:45.638
-Your mother lived
a long, interesting life.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.706
-Yes.
-She recently passed away.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.410
-But you -- As a very good son,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.946
you helped her have
a social-media footprint.

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:55.848
-So, my mom died
when she was 95,

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:57.350
but, like,
around the time she was 91,

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.518
she was getting
a little depressed.

00:01:58.518 --> 00:01:59.819
A lot of her friends were dying.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.088
And she wasn't -- She works
as an artist, as well,

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.223
and she wasn't painting.

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.523
So I thought,
"How am I going to, like,

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:05.691
get her motivated again?"

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.426
So I introduced her
to Instagram,

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:09.328
which she thought was,
like, magic.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:10.662
-And she --
At first, she was really,

00:02:10.662 --> 00:02:12.631
"Sheesh, like, why would anyone
want to follow me?"

00:02:12.631 --> 00:02:14.567
I was like, "Trust me,
there are some people."

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.301
And I got her on Instagram.

00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:18.638
And then I created an account
for her artwork to, like,

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.005
get her out of bed and paint.

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:22.007
And she was like,
"Well, who's going to deal with

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:23.443
the customers?"

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:25.612
And there really wasn't anyone
to deal with the customers,

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:27.880
so I was like,
"I know what you're asking.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.381
I'll do it."

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:33.253
So I -- My mom was like, "Well,
you should invent a character.

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:34.687
You can't do it as yourself,

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.822
because you can't be like,
'Hi, I'm Anderson.

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.190
What size frame do you want?'"

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.259
-Right, right.

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.393
-So, my mom took
great pleasure in this,

00:02:41.393 --> 00:02:44.230
and she was like,
"Okay, why don't we invent

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.899
a lady of a certain age
named Monica

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:49.569
and who's a long-time
trusted assistant.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.870
And you be Monica and you answer

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.875
all the DMs on Instagram
to buy my artwork."

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:56.876
And that's what I did
for three years.

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:57.877
-Wow!

00:02:57.877 --> 00:03:01.547
-Yeah, I would be --
-Very well done.

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:07.286
-I would be -- Literally, I'd be
in Baghdad, and, you know,

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:09.655
in between things, I'd be like,

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:13.226
"Would you like
a laminated white frame?"

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:14.326
Yeah, as Monica.

00:03:14.326 --> 00:03:15.461
And people would be like,

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.729
"What's it like working for
Ms. Vanderbilt?"

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:20.533
I was like,
"Oh, she's so lovely."

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.034
I don't know why
she picked the name.

00:03:22.034 --> 00:03:24.136
I don't know who -- Yeah, I was
like, "Couldn't it be a guy?"

00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:25.538
But, apparently,
it couldn't have been.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:27.673
-I like, as soon as she got
involved with social media,

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:31.044
she realized it was
a good platform to lie on.

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:32.512
-Yes.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.580
-I'll actually
use that as a transition.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.382
You've done
something fascinating.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:40.119
You have actually
interacted with some people

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:42.421
who got very deep
in this QAnon world,

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:44.657
to the point that they had

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:47.527
and embraced
conspiracy theories about you.

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:48.594
Like, some
really horrifying things.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:49.928
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:49.928 --> 00:03:52.098
-And how did you end up reaching
out and talking to one?

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:54.133
-Well, one of them --
There was a person in

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:56.636
a documentary that's, you know,
was doing about QAnon,

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.104
and there's this guy --
I think he was in Australia --

00:03:59.104 --> 00:04:01.306
who believed that I ate babies.

00:04:01.306 --> 00:04:04.043
-Yeah.
-And -- Currently.

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:10.116
And -- But, yeah, and that was
just one of the QAnon beliefs.

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:11.951
You know, I mean, it's just --
it's insane.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.386
It's based on age-old, like,

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:16.556
anti-Semitic tropes
and anti-Catholic tropes,

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:18.024
the whole thing of,
like, you know,

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:20.025
blood-libel Jews
eating the blood --

00:04:20.025 --> 00:04:21.627
you know, drinking the blood
of children.

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:22.695
The Nazis said that.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.097
That's what QAnon believes,
as well,

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:26.933
but it's all celebrities
and people on television.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.302
And, yeah, so,
I interviewed the guy.

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:32.705
'cause he's now -- The first guy
had now renounced his beliefs,

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:35.541
and I wanted to know like,
"Did you genuinely believe this,

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:38.044
that I, like,
was eating babies?"

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:39.879
And he was like, "Yeah.
Yeah, I did."

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.748
And he was like, "I'm sorry.
You seem like a nice guy."

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:43.081
I was like, "Well, thanks."

00:04:43.081 --> 00:04:45.417
I was like, "No, don't make
any definite decisions.

00:04:45.417 --> 00:04:46.486
See how, you know --"

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:48.253
-I mean, those moments
must be nice,

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:49.722
but, obviously, they're
few and far between, right?

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:52.291
-Like, I feel like there's not a
wave of people renouncing QAnon.

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:53.592
-No, no, no.

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:55.761
I've interviewed another guy
who reached out to me

00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:58.665
on direct message to tell me
that I was going to be executed.

00:04:58.665 --> 00:05:00.866
I think it was last March
in Washington.

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:03.135
And I was like,
"Does he really think this?"

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:04.469
And so I actually reached out.

00:05:04.469 --> 00:05:07.539
I was like, "You know, how do
you -- Why that date?"

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:09.275
I was just sort of curious.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:10.610
-And, anyway,
we started talking.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.712
And he seemed
interested in talking,

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:13.846
and I ended up interviewing him.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:15.347
And it was fascinating.

00:05:15.347 --> 00:05:17.149
You know, he had a family.
He had a life.

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:19.985
And he believes
the Vatican has fallen

00:05:19.985 --> 00:05:21.120
and the pope has been arrested

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.888
and that Joe Biden is
not president,

00:05:22.888 --> 00:05:25.424
but it's somebody in a costume
that looks like Joe Biden.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:26.558
I -- Yeah.

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:28.127
-Well, I'm looking forward
to finding out

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.328
which one of you guys is right.

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:32.131
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:34.200
Hey, thanks for being here.
Congrats on the book.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.434
-Thanks.
-It's all ways nice to see you.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:38.237
"Anderson Cooper 360"
airs weeknights on CNN,

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:39.572
and "Vanderbilt: The Rise
and Fall of an American Dynasty"

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:40.573
is out now.

